        "headingId": 498,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:11:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 520,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 499,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73136,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 163,
        "headingId": 499,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2B32 Immunodeficiency-associated lymphoproliferative disorders</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:11:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 521,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 500,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73137,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 163,
        "headingId": 500,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> AIDS-related diffuse large B-cell lymphoma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:11:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 522,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 501,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73138,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 163,
        "headingId": 501,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:11:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 523,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 502,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73139,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 163,
        "headingId": 502,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Immunodeficiency-associated CNS lymphomas typically manifest in the CNS parenchyma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:11:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 524,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 503,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73140,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 163,
        "headingId": 503,
        "headingText": "<p>The clinical presentation and imaging features of immunodeficiency-associated CNS lymphoma may be similar to those of CNS lymphoma in immunocompetent patients. Multiple lesions and areas of necrosis occur more frequently in immunodeficiency-associated CNS lymphoma than in CNS lymphoma of immunocompetent patients {21818113}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2021-10-22T11:10:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 525,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 504,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73141,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 163,
        "headingId": 504,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Immunodeficiency-associated CNS lymphomas may develop in rare hereditary immunodeficiency syndromes or (more commonly) in acquired immunodeficiency conditions related to infectious, autoimmune, or neoplastic diseases, or to immunosuppressive therapies. AIDS-related primary diffuse large B-cell lymphoma of the CNS (CNS-DLBCL) has become less common with the introduction of highly active antiretroviral therapy (HAART) {21915121}. EBV-positive DLBCL of the elderly is related to immunosenescence and occurs in patients aged &gt;&nbsp;50&nbsp;years {17785567}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:11:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 526,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 505,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73142,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 163,
        "headingId": 505,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Immunodeficiency syndromes underlying immunodeficiency-associated CNS lymphoma include ataxia telangiectasia, Wiskott&ndash;Aldrich syndrome, and IgA deficiency. Other underlying conditions include autoimmune disorders (e.g. systemic lupus erythematosus and Sj&ouml;gren syndrome), neoplastic diseases, and iatrogenic immunosuppression (either for the purpose of organ transplantation or due to treatment with immunosuppressive drugs). Infectious disorders such as HIV and HTLV infections that lead to immunodeficiency also increase the risk of CNS lymphomas, as does immunosenescence.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:11:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 527,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 506,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73143,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 163,
        "headingId": 506,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>EBV infection is important because most immunodeficiency-associated lymphomas are EBV-related.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:11:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 534,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 513,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73144,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 163,
        "headingId": 513,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Multifocal presentation is more frequent than in CNS lymphomas in immunocompetent patients, as is a tendency to contain more and larger areas of necrosis. Tumours may simulate necrotizing cerebral toxoplasmosis, which may occur concomitantly {15311360}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:11:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 528,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 507,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73145,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 163,
        "headingId": 507,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Immunodeficiency-associated CNS lymphomas are typically EBV-related, so in addition to B-cell markers such as CD19, CD20, and CD79a, the lymphoma cells express the EBV-associated proteins EBNA1&ndash;6, LMP1, and EBV-encoded small RNAs&nbsp;1 and 2 (EBER1 and EBER2) {10071343}.</p>\r\n<p>Otherwise, AIDS-related CNS-DLBCL shares the characteristics of CNS-DLBCL in immunocompetent patients.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:11:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 529,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 508,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73146,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 163,
        "headingId": 508,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cytology is of limited value for immunodeficiency-associated CNS lymphomas.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:11:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 530,
    "bookid": 45,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 509,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 73147,
        "bookId": 45,
        "chapterId": 163,
        "headingId": 509,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Demonstration of a clonal B-cell population may be helpful in diagnostically ambiguous cases.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2021-09-30T16:11:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
